<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page3-elimination-and-threats" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Elimination Revisited, and Threats and Promises Again</title>

  <p>
    Back on the first page of this chapter we noticed something in passing. In
    the Prisoner's Dilemma, <m>\text{talk}</m> strictly dominates
    <m>\text{refuse}</m> for both executives, so eliminating dominated
    strategies would have landed on <m>(\text{talk}, \text{talk})</m>, which is
    exactly the Nash equilibrium we found by inspection. That was left as an
    observation. Section 3.6 turns it into a theorem, and tells us how far it
    can be pushed.
  </p>

  <p>
    This matters more than it might look. We spent all of Chapter 2 eliminating
    dominated strategies without ever saying what the surviving strategies
    were <em>for</em>. The answer, it turns out, is that they are where the
    Nash equilibria live, at least if we are careful about which kind of
    dominance we used.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-6">
    <title>Section 3.6, Finding Nash Equilibria by Iterated Elimination of
    Dominated Strategies</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 3.6 up through the end of the paragraph beginning
      <q>In contrast,</q> just after Theorem 3.2.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      That gets you both theorem statements and the discussion around them,
      which is all we will use. The rest of the section is the proof.
      <alert>You may skip the proof entirely.</alert> Read it if you are
      interested, and do come back to it if you ever want to see how an
      induction on <q>the <m>k</m>th strategy eliminated</q> is set up, but
      nothing on this page or in your homework depends on it.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Let's collect what those two theorems actually say. Both of them start the
    same way: you take a game <m>G</m>, run iterated elimination on it, and
    call the smaller game you end up with <m>H</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Theorem 3.1 covers <em>weak</em> elimination. It says that every Nash
    equilibrium of <m>H</m> is still a Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>, and in
    particular, if <m>H</m> is a single strategy profile, then that profile is
    a Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>. That second part is just the statement that
    a dominant strategy equilibrium is a Nash equilibrium.
  </p>

  <p>
    Theorem 3.2 covers <em>strict</em> elimination, and it says considerably
    more. Statement (1) is that the order of elimination does not matter; you
    reach the same <m>H</m> no matter how you get there. Statement (2) is that
    any strategy you eliminated along the way was not part of any Nash
    equilibrium of <m>G</m> to begin with. Statement (3) is the same
    conclusion as Theorem 3.1, that equilibria of <m>H</m> are equilibria of
    <m>G</m>. And statement (4) says that if <m>H</m> is a single strategy
    profile, that profile is a strict Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>, and there
    are no others.
  </p>

  <p>
    Statement (1) should feel familiar. Back on the last page of Chapter 2 we
    worked out by hand why an elimination that is available under strict
    dominance stays available, and why that is exactly what fails for weak
    dominance. That argument is what statement (1) is asserting in general.
  </p>

  <p>
    Statement (2) is the genuinely new one, and its idea is worth having even
    though you are skipping the proof. Suppose <m>s_i</m> was eliminated
    because <m>t_i</m> strictly dominates it, and suppose <m>s_i</m> shows up
    somewhere in a strategy profile. Swap <m>s_i</m> for <m>t_i</m> and leave
    everyone else alone. Player <m>i</m>'s payoff goes up, so that profile was
    never a Nash equilibrium. Notice where strictness earns its keep: with weak
    dominance that swap only gets you <m>\geq</m>, and <m>\geq</m> is precisely
    what a Nash equilibrium is allowed to have.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now here is the punchline, which the book states in words rather than as a
    numbered statement, so it is easy to read past. Put statement (2) together
    with statement (3):
  </p>

  <p>
    <em>If you use iterated elimination of strictly dominated strategies, then
    <m>G</m> and <m>H</m> have exactly the same Nash equilibria.</em>
  </p>

  <p>
    One direction is statement (3) itself. For the other direction, take a Nash
    equilibrium <m>s^*</m> of <m>G</m>. By statement (2) none of its pieces
    <m>s_i^*</m> were ever eliminated, so <m>s^*</m> is still a strategy
    profile of the smaller game <m>H</m>. And it is still an equilibrium there
    for a slightly boring reason: <m>H</m> gives each player <em>fewer</em>
    strategies to deviate to than <m>G</m> did, so a profile that survived
    every deviation in <m>G</m> certainly survives the shorter list in
    <m>H</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    So for strict elimination, shrinking the game costs you nothing at all. Find
    the equilibria of <m>H</m> and you have found every equilibrium of
    <m>G</m>. This is the practical reason we bother eliminating in the first
    place.
  </p>

  <p>
    For weak elimination, only one direction survives. Nash equilibria of
    <m>H</m> are still equilibria of <m>G</m>, so nothing false gets
    introduced, but equilibria of <m>G</m> can be destroyed on the way down.
    <alert>Weak elimination can lose you equilibria; strict elimination
    cannot.</alert> Keep that sentence somewhere handy, because it is the one
    result from this section you will actually use.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-strict-vs-weak-loss">
    <statement>
      <p>
        You run iterated elimination on a game <m>G</m> and get a reduced game
        <m>H</m>. What is the difference between having used strict dominance
        and having used weak dominance?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Either way, every Nash equilibrium of <m>H</m> is one of
            <m>G</m>. But with weak dominance, <m>G</m> may have equilibria
            that no longer appear in <m>H</m>, while with strict dominance it
            cannot.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Both theorems give you the <q>nothing false is
            introduced</q> direction. Only strict dominance gives you the
            <q>nothing is lost</q> direction, and that is statement (2) of
            Theorem 3.2 doing the work.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            With weak dominance, <m>H</m> may contain a Nash equilibrium that
            was not a Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite, and this is the direction that is safe in both cases.
            Theorem 3.1 says explicitly that every Nash equilibrium of <m>H</m>
            is also one of <m>G</m>, weak dominance included. The danger runs
            the other way: an equilibrium of <m>G</m> can go missing.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            With weak dominance you can only eliminate one strategy per player,
            while with strict dominance you can keep going.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Nothing limits how many strategies you can eliminate in
            either case. We eliminated repeatedly with weak dominance all
            through Chapter 2.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            With weak dominance the payoffs in the surviving cells change,
            which is why the equilibria can differ.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Eliminating a strategy just deletes a row or a column,
            and every payoff still showing is exactly what it was before. What
            changes is which comparisons you are still able to make.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    The next check is about reading statement (2) carefully, since it is phrased
    in terms of a single player's strategy rather than a whole profile.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-strategy-vs-profile">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Statement (2) of Theorem 3.2 says that each strategy eliminated in the
        course of iterated elimination of strictly dominated strategies is not
        part of any Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>. What does that let you
        conclude about a strategy profile <m>s = (s_1, \ldots, s_n)</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            If even one player's strategy <m>s_i</m> was eliminated, then the
            whole profile <m>s</m> fails to be a Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>,
            no matter what the other players are doing.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. A single eliminated component is enough to disqualify the
            entire profile, which is why statement (2) is such a strong claim.
            It is also what lets us conclude that every Nash equilibrium of
            <m>G</m> is built entirely out of surviving strategies, and so is
            still sitting there in <m>H</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If every player's strategy <m>s_i</m> was eliminated, then <m>s</m>
            fails to be a Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>. If only some were
            eliminated, the statement says nothing.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite, and this reads the statement as weaker than it is. The
            claim is about each eliminated strategy on its own: any profile
            containing it is disqualified. One bad component is enough.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>s</m> survives to the reduced game <m>H</m>, then <m>s</m> is
            a Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That is not statement (2), and it is not true either. Surviving
            elimination does not make a profile an equilibrium; plenty of
            profiles in <m>H</m> are not equilibria of anything. Statement (3)
            is the one about profiles, and it applies to Nash equilibria of
            <m>H</m>, not to every profile of <m>H</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            An eliminated strategy <m>s_i</m> is never a best response to
            anything, so no player would ever use it.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This is stronger than what statement (2) claims, and it is a
            different kind of statement. Statement (2) rules out eliminated
            strategies appearing in Nash equilibria; it does not talk about
            best responses at all. We will get to best responses in the next
            section.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-single-profile-conclusion">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose iterated elimination of <em>strictly</em> dominated strategies
        reduces a game <m>G</m> all the way down to a single strategy profile
        <m>(s_1^*, \ldots, s_n^*)</m>. What can you conclude?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            It is a strict Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>, and <m>G</m> has no
            other Nash equilibria.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct, and this is the best possible outcome of a reduction. The
            <q>no others</q> half comes from statement (2): any other
            equilibrium would have to use a strategy that got eliminated, and
            those cannot appear in equilibria at all.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It is a Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>, but <m>G</m> may well have
            others that the elimination missed.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This is the right conclusion for <em>weak</em> elimination, not
            strict. Under strict elimination nothing gets missed, since
            statement (2) rules out the eliminated strategies appearing in any
            equilibrium.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It is the unique Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>, though it need not
            be a strict one.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Uniqueness is right, but you get strictness for free here as well.
            Every alternative strategy was eliminated, and the comparisons
            behind a strict elimination are all strict inequalities.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It is a Nash equilibrium of the reduced game <m>H</m>, but you
            cannot say anything about <m>G</m> without checking directly.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Transferring conclusions from <m>H</m> back to <m>G</m>
            is the entire purpose of these two theorems, so that you do not
            have to go back and check the original game by hand.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Now we put the theorems to work on a game we know very well, and something
    interesting happens.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-7">
    <title>Section 3.7, Big Monkey and Little Monkey 4: Threats, Promises,
    and Commitments Revisited</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 3.7.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a short section with no new machinery in it, and it closes a loop
      we opened all the way back in Section 1.6. It uses the same normal form
      matrix you worked with in Chapter 2, and both of the elimination orders
      you stepped through there. Read it properly rather than skimming; the
      payoff is conceptual rather than computational.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Here is the matrix again for reference, with Big Monkey moving first, as it
    appeared in Section 2.10 and in the two elimination interactives from
    Chapter 2.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-bmlm-normal-form-3-7">
    <title>Big Monkey and Little Monkey in normal form, Big Monkey first</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Little Monkey: ww</cell>
        <cell>Little Monkey: wc</cell>
        <cell>Little Monkey: cw</cell>
        <cell>Little Monkey: cc</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Big Monkey: w</cell>
        <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
        <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
        <cell>(9, 1)</cell>
        <cell>(9, 1)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Big Monkey: c</cell>
        <cell>(4, 4)</cell>
        <cell>(5, 3)</cell>
        <cell>(4, 4)</cell>
        <cell>(5, 3)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    In Chapter 2 we ran iterated elimination of weakly dominated strategies on
    this matrix in two different orders. The first order took us all the way
    down to the single profile <m>(w, cw)</m>. The second order stopped at a
    <m>2 \times 1</m> game, leaving both <m>(w, cw)</m> and <m>(w, cc)</m>
    standing. At the time, the point was that the two orders disagreed. Now we
    have the vocabulary to say what that disagreement cost us.
  </p>

  <p>
    Since these were weak eliminations, Theorem 3.1 applies and Theorem 3.2
    does not. So we know that <m>(w, cw)</m> and <m>(w, cc)</m> really are Nash
    equilibria of the original game, and we know we have no guarantee of having
    found them all. And in fact we have not: <m>(c, ww)</m> is a third Nash
    equilibrium, and no order of weak elimination will ever produce it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Those three profiles are worth naming in words, because we have met all of
    them before under different descriptions.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>(w, cw)</m> is the backward induction outcome from Chapter 1. Big
        Monkey waits, Little Monkey climbs, and the payoffs are <m>(9, 1)</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>(w, cc)</m> contains Little Monkey's <em>promise</em> from Section
        1.6: she will climb even if Big Monkey climbs. The payoffs are again
        <m>(9, 1)</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>(c, ww)</m> contains Little Monkey's <em>threat</em> from Section
        1.6: she will wait even if Big Monkey waits. The payoffs are
        <m>(4, 4)</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Look at the payoffs in that list. The threat changes the outcome of the
    game and the promise does not, which is exactly what Section 1.6 told us
    without the language of Nash equilibria. Hold onto that; we will come back
    to it at the bottom of the page.
  </p>

  <p>
    The book verifies <m>(c, ww)</m> for us in words, and its argument has two
    halves, one per player. If Little Monkey commits in advance to waiting no
    matter what Big Monkey does, then Big Monkey can do no better than to
    climb. And if Big Monkey climbs, then Little Monkey can do no better than
    to wait, which is what <m>ww</m> has her do. Those two sentences together
    are the definition of a Nash equilibrium applied to this profile.
  </p>

  <p>
    You are going to write the same kind of argument for the other two
    equilibria. Work each one out before revealing the answer.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="verify-w-cc">
    <title>Verifying the promise, <m>(w, cc)</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write the two-sentence argument showing that <m>(w, cc)</m> is a Nash
        equilibrium: one sentence about Big Monkey's incentive, one about
        Little Monkey's. Use the same shape as the book's argument for
        <m>(c, ww)</m>, and cite the payoffs from the matrix above.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Check one player at a time, holding the other one fixed. For Big
        Monkey, read down the <m>cc</m> column and compare his two payoffs. For
        Little Monkey, read across the <m>w</m> row and compare her four.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        If Little Monkey commits in advance to climbing no matter what Big
        Monkey does, then Big Monkey can do no better than to wait: waiting
        pays him <m>9</m> and climbing pays him <m>5</m>. And if Big Monkey
        waits, Little Monkey can do no better than to climb, which is what
        <m>cc</m> has her do: across the <m>w</m> row her payoffs are
        <m>0, 0, 1, 1</m>, and <m>cc</m> gives her one of the two best.
      </p>
      <p>
        Notice the <q>one of the two best.</q> Little Monkey ties with
        <m>cw</m> rather than beating it, so her half of the argument only
        gives <m>\geq</m>. That is all the definition asks for, but it does
        mean this equilibrium is not a strict one.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="verify-w-cw">
    <title>Verifying backward induction's answer, <m>(w, cw)</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Now do the same for <m>(w, cw)</m>. Then answer a second question:
        comparing your argument to the one you just wrote for <m>(w, cc)</m>,
        what actually changed and what did not?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Big Monkey is now looking down the <m>cw</m> column instead of the
        <m>cc</m> column. Little Monkey is reading across the same row she was
        before.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        If Little Monkey commits in advance to <m>cw</m>, then Big Monkey can
        do no better than to wait: waiting pays him <m>9</m> and climbing pays
        him <m>4</m>. And if Big Monkey waits, Little Monkey can do no better
        than <m>cw</m>: across the <m>w</m> row her payoffs are
        <m>0, 0, 1, 1</m>, and <m>cw</m> gives her one of the two best.
      </p>
      <p>
        What changed: exactly one number. Big Monkey's alternative was
        <m>5</m> against <m>cc</m> and is <m>4</m> against <m>cw</m>. Either
        way it loses to <m>9</m>, so the conclusion was never in doubt.
      </p>
      <p>
        What did not change: Little Monkey's half of the argument, at all. It
        is the same row, the same four payoffs <m>0, 0, 1, 1</m>, and the same
        tie between <m>cw</m> and <m>cc</m>. We are pointing at a different one
        of two cells that hold the same number.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    That last observation deserves a name. <m>(w, cw)</m> and <m>(w, cc)</m>
    are two genuinely different strategy profiles, and yet they pay both
    players identically and their verifications are the same argument twice.
    Something must distinguish them, so what is it?
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-off-path">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the actual difference between Little Monkey's strategies
        <m>cw</m> and <m>cc</m>, given that Big Monkey is playing <m>w</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            They differ only in what Little Monkey would do if Big Monkey
            climbed. Big Monkey does not climb, so that part of her strategy is
            never carried out and the two strategies produce identical play.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct, and this is the heart of Section 3.7. A strategy has to
            specify a move at <em>every</em> node, including nodes the game
            never actually reaches. Little Monkey's promise costs her nothing
            precisely because it is never tested. This is also the honest
            answer to the book's question of why the normal form has equilibria
            that backward induction does not find, and it is an idea we will
            return to in a later chapter.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            One of them is a strict Nash equilibrium and the other is not.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Neither is strict, in fact. In both cases Little Monkey ties: her
            payoff in the <m>w</m> row is <m>1</m> for <m>cw</m> and <m>1</m>
            for <m>cc</m>, so switching between them costs her nothing. That
            tie is the reason neither equilibrium is strict.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Little Monkey prefers one of them, so she would choose that one if
            given the chance.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            She is indifferent. Both give her <m>1</m> when Big Monkey waits.
            If she strictly preferred one of them, the other could not have
            been a Nash equilibrium in the first place.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            They differ in what Little Monkey does when Big Monkey waits, which
            is the branch that actually gets played.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite; look at the labels again. Both <m>cw</m> and <m>cc</m>
            begin with <m>c</m>, which is Little Monkey's move in response to
            Big Monkey waiting. It is the second letter, her response to Big
            Monkey climbing, where they part ways.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    One more thing worth noticing before we move on, since it will matter on
    the next page. None of these three equilibria is strict, and Little Monkey
    is the reason every time. In the <m>w</m> row she is indifferent between
    <m>cw</m> and <m>cc</m>; in the <m>c</m> row she is indifferent between
    <m>ww</m> and <m>cw</m>. She is never made strictly worse off by deviating.
  </p>

  <p>
    Notice in particular that <m>cw</m> is among her best replies in
    <em>both</em> of Big Monkey's rows. Keep an eye on that when we start
    drawing boxes in Section 3.9.
  </p>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-3-3" iframe="question-form.html?section=3.3" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
